{
  "query": "LumaPay runs a globally available service on Cloud Spanner and most customers are in Germany and Singapore, and leadership requires the lowest possible latency for both reads and writes for users in those areas. Which deployment approach in Spanner should you implement to satisfy this requirement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Build two independent Spanner instances in europe-west3 and asia-southeast1 and keep data in sync using Cloud Dataflow",
      "explanation": "This creates separate databases in each continent and uses a data pipeline to move changes between them.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use a global HTTP(S) load balancer to steer clients to the nearest Spanner endpoint",
      "explanation": "This places a layer 7 load balancer in front of endpoints so traffic is routed by proximity rather than changing the database configuration.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Provision a multi region Spanner instance that spans Europe and Asia and use it for the application",
      "explanation": "This chooses a Spanner configuration with replicas across both continents so clients can access a nearby replica for reads and participate in distributed writes.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Place a single region Spanner instance in europe-west1 and route all traffic from Asia to it",
      "explanation": "This centralizes the database in one region and forwards requests from other continents across long distances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Provision a multi region Spanner instance that spans Europe and Asia and use it for the application</b>.</p><p>This approach lets Spanner place replicas close to users in both Germany and Singapore so that reads can be served locally while writes commit through a geographically distributed quorum that preserves strong consistency. It also provides automatic failover and synchronous replication which together deliver low latency and high availability for a globally used service.</p><p><i>Build two independent Spanner instances in europe-west3 and asia-southeast1 and keep data in sync using Cloud Dataflow</i> is not correct because separate databases cannot offer strongly consistent reads and writes and a Dataflow pipeline would only provide eventual consistency with complex conflict handling rather than transactional replication.</p><p><i>Use a global HTTP(S) load balancer to steer clients to the nearest Spanner endpoint</i> is not correct because Spanner is accessed through client libraries and gRPC endpoints and is not fronted by an external HTTP load balancer. Steering traffic at the HTTP layer would not change Spanner’s replication or commit behavior and would not reduce write latency across continents.</p><p><i>Place a single region Spanner instance in europe-west1 and route all traffic from Asia to it</i> is not correct because users in Singapore would experience high latency for both reads and writes which fails the requirement for the lowest possible latency in both locations.</p>",
